(i) Suppose that $\ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}}$ has a non-empty interior with positive probability. Then we have  egin{eqnarray*} 0 &<& \ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(\ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}} 	ext{ has non-empty interior}) \ &=& \ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(\exists n, \exists \ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n: C(\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n) \subset \ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}}) \ &\leq& \sum_{ n, \ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n} \ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(C(\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n) \subset \ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}}). \end{eqnarray*} Since we sum over countably many cubes, there must exist $n$ and $\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n$ such that $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(C(\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n) \subset \ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}}) > 0$. Hence, by translation invariance, $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(C(\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n) \subset \ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}}) > 0$ for this specific $n$ and all $\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n$. We can apply the FKG inequality to obtain $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(\ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}} = [0,1]^d) = \ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(C(\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n) \subset \ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}} \, orall \ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_1,\ldots,\ensuremath{\mathbf{i}}_n) >0$. Since $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(\ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}} = [0,1]^d) = \prod_{n=1}^{\infty} p_n^{N^{dn}}$, this proves the first part of the theorem. (ii) Suppose $\prod_{n=1}^{\infty} p_n^{N^n} > 0$. Then for each $x\in [0,1]^{d-1}$ we have $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(\{x\} 	imes [0,1] \subset \ensuremath{D_{	ext{fat}}}) \geq \prod_{n=1}^{\infty} p_n^{N^n} > 0$. Let $\lambda_{d-1}$ denote $(d-1)$-dimensional Lebesgue measure. Applying Fubini's theorem gives  egin{eqnarray*} && \ensuremath{\mathbb{E}} \lambda_{d-1}(\{x\in [0,1]^{d-1} :